course: dictionary
lesson: 5452
title: Landmark: a word with three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A landmark is something easy to see and remember, which helps you find your way. «A landmark is something easy to see and remember, like a tall tower, a statue, a bridge, a river or a big red door.» The context tells you what the word means, so you can understand it without a dictionary.
explain: Read the words around it to choose the right meaning. «Pick a landmark at each turn» means a place you can spot easily, so you can find your way back. «A famous landmark» means a well-known building or place that many people recognise.
explain: The word can also mean an important event or change. A landmark decision is one that changes things a lot. To check which meaning fits, swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A landmark is a building or place that is easy to see and recognise.
fact: A famous landmark is known to many people, like Big Ben in the UK or the Statue of Liberty in the USA.
fact: Landmarks help with directions: «turn left at the church» uses the church as a landmark.
fact: A landmark event is an important event that changes things, like a landmark decision.
fact: Even tiny animals use landmarks: in tests, «bees learned to count landmarks up to about four to find sugar water».
exercise: «When you go somewhere new, pick a landmark at each turn.» What is a landmark in this sentence? | *A place or thing that is easy to see and remember, to help you find the way | A type of road | A map of the whole country | A new kind of car
exercise: Which of these is a famous landmark in Australia? | The Statue of Liberty | The CN Tower | *Uluru | Big Ben
exercise: «The court made a landmark decision that changed the law.» What does landmark mean here? | *An important event or change | A tall tower that people can climb | A place where people meet to play | A small sign on a road
test: «If you are lost, look for a landmark and remember it.» What should you do? | Run quickly in any direction | Close your eyes and wait | *Look for a landmark and remember it | Stay still and shout for help
test: Which sentence uses landmark to mean a famous building or place? | The bridge was painted last week | The landmark was very noisy | *The Golden Gate Bridge is a famous landmark | I crossed the road at the lights
exam: Jayden says a landmark is a kind of map. Is he right? | Yes, a landmark is a road sign | No, a landmark is only a decision | Yes, a landmark is a map of a town | *No, a landmark is a building, place or event that is easy to recognise
